  <h1>What to Do After Pregnancy Discrimination</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>1. Document the timeline</h2><p>Write down the exact sequence: when you announced the pregnancy, every employment action that followed, what was said, who was present. A clear timeline connecting the pregnancy announcement to adverse actions is your strongest evidence.</p>
  <h2>2. Identify comparators</h2><p>Note how non-pregnant employees with similar roles, performance, or tenure were treated. If a non-pregnant colleague on light duty was accommodated and you were not, that is direct evidence of unequal treatment under the PDA.</p>
  <h2>3. File an EEOC charge</h2><p>Must be filed within 180 days (300 days in states with fair employment agencies) of each discriminatory act. File at eeoc.gov or at your nearest EEOC office. The charge preserves your right to sue. Request a right-to-sue letter if the EEOC hasn&rsquo;t acted within 180 days of your charge.</p>
  <h2>4. If still employed: request PWFA accommodation in writing</h2><p>Submit a written request for reasonable accommodation of your pregnancy-related limitation to HR. The employer is required to engage in an interactive process. Keep copies of all communications. Retaliation for making an accommodation request is independently actionable.</p>
  <h2>5. Consult an employment attorney</h2><p>Most employment attorneys take Title VII pregnancy discrimination cases on contingency. Contact one early — before the EEOC charge deadline and before memories fade.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-pregnancy-discrimination-damages-work">how damages work</a> and <a href="/guides/pregnancy-discrimination-misconceptions">common misconceptions</a>.</p>
